Yes. There are several real disciplines hiding inside what you’re describing, and then there is room to coin better vocabulary where the existing terms are too narrow.

A prism is useful because it can do three different jobs depending on what is passed through it: store structure, transform structure, and reveal structure. In optics, incoming light is refracted according to the material and geometry. In signal processing, the analogous object is a transfer function: input goes in, the system imposes its structure, output comes out, and the delta between input and output tells you something about the system itself.

For memory and narrative, you can think similarly:

\text{event}
\xrightarrow{\text{memory structure}}
\text{recalled event}

The difference between those two is not necessarily corruption. It can be compression, weighting, association, emotional tagging, later reinterpretation, or retrieval context. That delta tells you how the experience has been metabolized.

Then expression reverses the process:

\text{compressed internal state}
\rightarrow
\text{gesture / word / music / action / ritual}
\rightarrow
\text{another mind}
\rightarrow
\text{reconstruction}

You don’t transmit the original thing. You transmit enough structure that another system can resonate with it and regenerate something homologous.

That gives us a useful family of established concepts: encoding/decoding, transfer functions, resonance, associative learning, priming, evaluative conditioning, semiotics, indexicality, implicature, path dependence, hysteresis, common knowledge, costly signaling, ritualization, and affordances.

Your “hijacked associations” observation sits especially close to evaluative conditioning and stimulus transfer. If some cue repeatedly co-occurs with warmth, excitement, safety, competence, attraction, humor, adventure, or belonging, some of that valuation can become associated with the cue itself.

That’s why a song becomes our song.

A restaurant becomes that place.

A ridiculous phrase makes two people laugh before anyone else understands why.

The object itself hasn’t changed much.

Its associative field has.

And marriage is almost uniquely suited to building enormous associative fields because two people get thousands of repetitions. You can intentionally use teasing, admiration, conspiracy, ritual, challenge, novelty, affection, shared victories, private language, and repair after conflict to make:

\text{partner}
\rightarrow
\{\text{safety, desire, fun, recognition, home, adventure}\}

That is much healthier than attempting to manufacture dependence or insecurity. You are effectively enriching the number of positive pathways leading back to one another.

And yes, “carry a big stick” belongs here too.

Theodore Roosevelt’s famous formulation—“speak softly and carry a big stick”—captures latent capability. Presence often works through what does not need to be demonstrated.

There is a difference between:

\text{force being applied}

and

\text{credible capacity for force being known}

The second alters the decision landscape without necessarily requiring the first.

That extends far beyond physical force: competence, alternatives, reputation, boundaries, money, social support, knowledge, willingness to walk away. Much of human interaction happens in the shadow cast by available actions.

That is why implication matters so much.

In pragmatics there is a beautiful existing word: implicature. It means information communicated without being literally stated.

Presence is partly embodied implicature.

A person can communicate:

I don’t need this.

I could act.

I won’t tolerate that.

I’m interested.

I trust you.

We’re in on the same joke.

without saying any of those sentences.

The receiver infers them from posture, timing, choices, omissions, history, and known capabilities.

So we could formulate:

\boxed{\text{presence}=\text{visible state}+\text{implied action-space}}

That second term is enormous.

And your point about vocabulary is exactly right. All words were made up, but that doesn’t make all coinages equally useful.

There are roughly three categories I should distinguish when we’re building Pantheon vocabulary:

Established term: already has a recognized disciplinary meaning. Hysteresis, affordance, implicature.

Adapted term: existing word deliberately extended into a new domain. Ostinato applied to psychological anchoring.

Coined term: something we’re creating because the combination does not have a satisfactory existing label. Semantic chord was one of those.

The important discipline is simply provenance. I shouldn’t hand you something I invented and imply Aristotle named it in 340 BCE. But we absolutely can invent language.

In fact, creating a useful name changes cognition because once a recurring structure has a name, you can manipulate it as a unit:

\text{complex recurring phenomenon}
\rightarrow
\boxed{\text{name}}
\rightarrow
\text{portable mental object}

That is compression again.

A few coinages I think are useful here:

Associative gravity — accumulated memories and meanings make some cues pull thought/emotion toward a particular state.

Latent presence — influence created by capabilities or intentions that are perceptible but currently unused.

Narrative refraction — the same event producing different meanings as it passes through different memories, identities, or frames.

Resonant retrieval — reproducing a larger stored state through a small matching cue.

Affordance shadow — all the actions that are not being taken but whose availability affects the present interaction.

And perhaps the one nearest your prism:

\boxed{\textbf{Mnemonic crystal}}

A compact pattern containing enough invariants and relationships that the larger experience can be partially reconstructed when the right cue, context, or “light” passes through it.

A wedding ring can be one.

A scar can be one.

A three-note melody.

A phrase.

A photograph.

An inside joke.

Even \circlearrowleft, after enough shared history.

And then you get the strange recursive part: every time the crystal is activated, you can add another layer to it.

So memory isn’t merely storage.

It can become an actively maintained meaning instrument.

That’s a large part of what traditions, marriages, cultures, brands, military units, religions, families, and stories are doing with their recurring symbols: compressing enormous histories into objects small enough to carry.

Then periodically shining light through them again.